WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.940
- I am here with Iliza Shlesinger.

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:02.773
Give it up.

00:00:02.773 --> 00:00:04.190
(Kelly cheers)
(Iliza cheers)

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:06.940
- Yeah, (indistinct).
(Iliza laughs)

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.670
My next guest was an NBA
superstar who retired

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:10.830
after 12 seasons.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.640
He's recently become one of
Twitter's most popular accounts

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.670
with all sorts of heartwarming
and inspirational stories,

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:17.700
funny stories as well.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.290
And he's earned millions
of retweets and followers

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:22.340
including pretty much every
celebrity you can name.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:23.370
I'm one of them.

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.863
Let's say hello to Rex Chapman, everybody.

00:00:25.863 --> 00:00:28.930
(drum rolls)
(audience cheering)

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:32.450
First of all, I love your
sweatshirt because I'm Texan.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:33.990
Yeah, I like it.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.350
And secondly, where are you calling from?

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.020
- Lexington, Kentucky.
- All right, South.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:40.120
All right, well meet Eliza.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:40.953
Have y'all met before?

00:00:40.953 --> 00:00:41.786
I didn't know if you had.

00:00:41.786 --> 00:00:43.500
- I just know you played for the Suns.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.333
- I know.
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:44.333 --> 00:00:49.270
- Yeah, I haven't met Iliza
but my daughter told me

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:51.800
I had to follow her so
I started following her

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.590
and now I'm a huge fan.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:54.880
- I love that.
- Thank you.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:56.290
- Daughter tells-
- I'm gonna have to follow

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:57.123
your daughter.

00:00:57.123 --> 00:00:59.330
That sounds weird but
you she has a handle.

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:02.140
- I am a stalker.
- I'll do that, great.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:04.440
- All right, well Rex, how did
this start because you didn't

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.810
set out to be popular on Twitter?

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.560
- No, I played basketball for a long time

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:12.282
and this just kinda...

00:01:12.282 --> 00:01:16.280
A couple of years ago I was
really frustrated with the toxic

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.170
social media and kind of wandered off

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:20.910
and I watched so much basketball.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:24.890
I saw this video of a
dolphin swimming out,

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:28.290
a paddle boarder swimming
in and a dolphin jumped up

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.240
and just hit the guy right in the chest

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.577
and stupid basketball term I said,

00:01:32.577 --> 00:01:34.620
"That's a charge, blocker charge."

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.700
Well, I just tweeted it
and people thought it was

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.473
kind of funny and then it grew from there.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.650
So yeah, I try to throw in
some feel-good stuff from time

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:47.260
to time to make people laugh.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:51.040
- I mean that dolphin
video never gets old-

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.410
- It didn't charge because
his feet were planted

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.130
on the surf board.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:56.750
His feet were planted.
(Rex mumbles)

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.570
Couldn't go the other way
'cause don't have feet.

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:00.120
- Oh my God, you know sports ball?

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.299
- I know so much about sports
people are always telling me-

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:03.670
- She does, his feet are set.

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:05.085
Look, that's a charge.
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:05.085 --> 00:02:06.309
Exactly, Iliza (indistinct).

00:02:06.309 --> 00:02:09.070
- I played YMCA basketball
so I know 'cause I charged

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:09.930
a lot.
- [Kelly] YMCA basketball.

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.700
I got a lot of red flags in
lacrosse so I just know about

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.350
the aggressive behavior.
- [Kelly] Oh my gosh.

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:15.760
I love that.
(audience laughs)

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.860
Well, Rex it's pretty cool
to find a way to make social

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.470
media help your mental
state instead of hurt it

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:23.790
because I'm gonna be honest with you,

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.830
I watched 'The Social Dilemma"
I don't know if you saw that

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:28.360
but it freaked me out.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:33.220
And I think that it is kind of
daunting the film in general

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:36.845
but at the same time I love
that you've used it to help

00:02:36.845 --> 00:02:38.180
with mental health.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:40.083
And how have you managed to do that?

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.380
- Like I mentioned at the
beginning I played basketball

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:46.290
forever and my last three
years of playing I had seven

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:49.560
orthopedic surgeries and
right when I finished playing,

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.310
I wasn't a drinker or a smoker,
nothing my whole career.

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:56.690
And the doctor gave me Oxycontin
and that set me on about

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:59.580
a 15-year battle with
painkiller addiction.

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:04.580
I had three rehab stints and
so finally about six years ago

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:07.890
I went to rehab for the third time.

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:09.660
It took that time.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:12.380
I've been off of painkillers
for about six years

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:17.380
and I know life is hard and
you know heck I lived in my

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:18.610
car for a little while.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:21.160
I made probably $40
million playing basketball

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:22.970
and since then I've lived in my car,

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:24.900
I've lived on friend's couches.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:29.900
So I have the greatest debt
of gratitude to so many

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:32.480
of my family and friends who
have supported me through

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:36.060
everything and I think with the
Twitter shoot I'm just happy

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.900
to be able to bring a
smile to somebody's face

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:42.080
and I am happy that so many
people share their videos

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:42.913
with me.

00:03:42.913 --> 00:03:45.820
None of the contents' mine,
they send it to me I put it out.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:49.210
- Yeah, well you have made me
smile a lot, you're hilarious.

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:52.010
I love the videos and they
do help whether they're sweet

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.506
or they make you laugh, all of it.

00:03:53.506 --> 00:03:57.443
You've also used Twitter to
help people financially as well?

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.610
- I think the coolest part
about this silly Twitter channel

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:04.060
has been right at the
beginning of the pandemic

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:07.462
I just turned the camera on
myself and put out a video

00:04:07.462 --> 00:04:09.987
to these Twitter followers and said,

00:04:09.987 --> 00:04:12.330
"Hey, look the times are
tough and people are gonna go

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.090
through tough times I believe."

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:19.090
And sure enough we've seen
the ravages of this pandemic

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:24.130
but within about a week
we had raised $300,000

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:27.450
and over the last eight or nine
months we've given out most

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:32.450
of that money in grants in New
York, New Jersey, California,

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:37.510
Phoenix, Charlotte for first
responders, food banks,

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:43.110
on and on just 'cause people
are really struggling, PPE.

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:47.760
So that's been really the best
part and most heartwarming

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.600
part about all of this Twitter
stuff is just that we've been

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.580
able to help some people.

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:55.780
I'm gonna continue to
try to do so this winter.

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:58.910
- Yeah, it's amazing to use
your spotlight for that.

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:00.110
It's really cool, man.
(audience claps)

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:03.390
As we have discussed, Rex is
a master at finding the most

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:06.130
heartwarming and inspirational
stories around to share

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:07.270
on his Twitter feed.

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:10.270
More than 1 million people
follow along to see what

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:11.640
he's gonna post next.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:14.610
I wanna share one of his more recent gems.

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:16.750
Rex, before we see it
give us a little setup.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:18.713
So what do you love about this video?

00:05:19.573 --> 00:05:24.120
- Oh man, you know my
my ex wife is a teacher,

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:28.510
my dad was a teacher, my
oldest daughter is a teacher

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:33.060
and I came across the
video last week or recently

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:35.680
that it was...

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.070
You know we've gone to
virtual learning online with

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:42.660
many of our classrooms across
the country and I came across

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:47.660
the sweetest video of some
students paying respect to their

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:50.679
teacher on the last day of school.

00:05:50.679 --> 00:05:52.536
- Oh, all right, let's roll it.

00:05:52.536 --> 00:05:53.860
(wind whooshing)

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:56.330
- [Brown] Seriously, is
it my fault that you have

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:57.180
your cameras off?

00:05:59.511 --> 00:06:03.160
- [Valentina] So Dr. Brown
we actually kind of wanted

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:05.462
to do something.

00:06:05.462 --> 00:06:07.123
- [Brown] With your camera's off?

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:10.983
- [Valentina] Everybody
if you wanna, go ahead.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:16.790
- [Brown] Oh, you guys.

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:20.677
You're gonna make me cry, no.
(mouth slurps)

00:06:24.245 --> 00:06:25.275
(wind whooshing)

00:06:25.275 --> 00:06:27.130
(audience claps)
- So sweet.

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:29.730
We actually have the
professor, Dr. James Brown

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:32.010
which is the coolest
doctor I've ever heard of,

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:33.810
Dr. James Brown I'm just saying.

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:36.170
He's on the line along with Valentina,

00:06:36.170 --> 00:06:38.720
the student who organized
this touching surprise.

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:42.475
Give it up for them y'all.
(audience cheering)

00:06:42.475 --> 00:06:47.140
So Dr. Brown you must have
been so moved when you realized

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:48.070
what was going on.

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:50.400
My mama was a teacher
and y'all worked so hard

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:55.400
and to actually have that
love reciprocated from your

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:57.863
students is pretty amazing, right?

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:02.280
- It was so emotional
and it was so emotional

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:03.890
for a variety of reasons.

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:07.660
Obviously I was very
touched by it as you can see

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:12.078
but we've been through so
much this semester together,

00:07:12.078 --> 00:07:17.078
COVID and the political
climate and then on top of that

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:20.607
I asked them to study some
really challenging subject

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:24.360
matter, the subject matter
is very challenging.

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:29.360
They're studying genocide and
atrocities and I was concerned

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:31.313
about them.

00:07:31.313 --> 00:07:34.520
I was concerned how they could handle this

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:37.660
and they did great.

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:40.940
They're so capable, they're so.

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:42.010
- Resilient.

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:43.580
- I'm so proud of them.

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:47.650
I'm so proud of what they
accomplished and some of that

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:51.520
emotion just spilled out
during that class session.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:54.370
- Yeah, well Valentina,
you actually came up with

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:55.530
the idea, right?

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:58.440
- Yeah, so recently there's
been a lot of trends on social

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:01.240
media of students showing
their professors and teachers

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:02.700
how much they care about them.

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:05.530
And Dr. Brown's amazing
and a few of my friends,

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:07.530
we came together and we
decided that we wanted to do

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:08.460
this for him.

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:11.690
So we private messaged our
whole class on zoom and we got

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:13.567
everybody in a group
chat and basically said,

00:08:13.567 --> 00:08:15.600
"Hey, we wanna do this for Dr. Brown.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:17.740
I know we've had a really
tough semester but he's always

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:20.580
there for us," and everyone
was on board right away

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:23.070
because everybody loves
him so much and has so much

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:24.380
respect for him.

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:27.500
- Oh that's, well 'cause
his name is Dr. James Brown

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:30.090
but I'm just saying it's a cool name.

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:34.190
What makes Dr. Brown so special
to your class, Valentina?

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:36.560
- He is one of the most
compassionate educators

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:37.820
I've honestly ever had.

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:41.152
He made our first semester
so memorable and especially

00:08:41.152 --> 00:08:43.670
because we're a class
of first year students

00:08:43.670 --> 00:08:46.970
and even online he's gone
above and beyond to make sure

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:50.440
that we feel safe and happy
and everything throughout

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:51.280
the class.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:53.980
In a regular year he even goes
as far as to invite students

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:55.950
to his house for Thanksgiving
if they're not going home

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:57.280
for the holidays.

00:08:57.280 --> 00:08:59.483
- Oh my God.
- [Iliza] That's so great.

00:08:59.483 --> 00:09:01.486
- Now, we're crying (indistinct).

00:09:01.486 --> 00:09:02.570
(audience laughs)

00:09:02.570 --> 00:09:05.670
Valentina, did you think
Dr. Brown would react

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:06.503
so emotionally?

00:09:06.503 --> 00:09:08.210
What did you think was gonna happen?

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:09.180
- Oh, not at all.

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:12.630
He's a very compassionate
man with a lot of empathy

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:15.070
and that's what he teaches
in class a lot too.

00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:17.660
So when he started to get
emotional I even got emotional

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:21.310
and a lot of people don't
think that teachers are in the

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:22.750
same position as students right now.

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:27.580
You know teachers and professors
are struggling just as much

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:30.110
as students are with the
virtual learning environment

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:32.760
and it's important that we're
showing them that we care

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:34.420
about them and that they're appreciated.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:37.430
- Was it weird to like
have this big outpour

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:38.827
and this emotional moment
and then he's like,

00:09:38.827 --> 00:09:41.438
"All right, let's get back to
our lesson plan on genocide,"

00:09:41.438 --> 00:09:42.799
if we can just lighten the mood.

00:09:42.799 --> 00:09:44.640
(audience laughs)

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:46.020
- [Kelly] Pick up for the semester.

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:47.763
What was that segway like?

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:53.230
No, I'm sitting here crying
because it's so beautiful

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:55.390
and like I said my mom was a
teacher and she did so much

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:57.860
and I can only imagine, I mean
Dr. Brown how they're talking

00:09:57.860 --> 00:10:01.060
about you, how Valentina
and she says her other peers

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:01.893
talk about you.

00:10:01.893 --> 00:10:03.320
It's such an important thing.

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:06.890
Teachers do so much and
they go above and beyond

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:09.640
and especially the great
teachers, especially the ones

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:13.330
like she said you're really
concerned and teaching them

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:16.690
to have empathy and character
and especially in such

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:17.830
a hard year.

00:10:17.830 --> 00:10:19.850
So anyway, thank you so much Dr. Brown

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:24.100
and thank you everyone for
taking the time to just make us

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:26.590
smile and make us cry
a little bit and laugh.

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:29.573
I love you, Iliza.
(Kelly laughs)

